     3,
     "On Thu, 2014-10-30 at 03:01 -0700, Tom Bird wrote:\r<br>&gt; Hi INLA,\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; I have a question about implementing the multinomial-poisson\r<br>&gt; transform  with a spatial model.   If I implement the following\r<br>&gt; multinomial approximation model:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; formula = y ~ -1 +\r<br>&gt;    ## one intercept pr multinom observation\r<br>&gt;    f(idx, model=&quot;iid&quot;,\r<br>&gt;     hyper = list(\r<br>&gt;        prec = list(\r<br>&gt;        initial = log(0.000001),\r<br>&gt;        fixed = TRUE))) +\r<br>&gt;     f(jdx, x, model=&quot;iid&quot;,\r<br>&gt;         ## beta&#39;s sum to zero?\r<br>&gt;         constr = sum.to.zero,\r<br>&gt;         hyper = list(\r<br>&gt;         prec = list(\r<br>&gt;         initial = log(0.001),\r<br>&gt;         fixed = TRUE)))\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Could one use the copy command to add a spatial random effect for each\r<br>&gt; of the jdx groups in the Multinomial?  for example using something\r<br>&gt; like that found in the DLM example\r<br>&gt;  (<a href=\"http://www.math.ntnu.no/inla/r-inla.org/papers/DLM_examples_2011-10-23.R\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.math.ntnu.no/inla/r-inla.org/papers/DLM_examples_2011-10-23.R&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792446011832000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0jGc-J1UkJ79Swm7zMuLvZ\">http://www.math.ntnu.no/inla/r-inla.org/papers/DLM_examples_2011-10-23.R</a>)?  Specifically, I&#39;m looking at this part, which provides the temporal link between observations:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;  + f(jdx, model=&quot;iid&quot;, initial=-10, fixed=T) +\r<br>&gt;    f(j, w1, copy=&quot;i&quot;)\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "Hi,\r<br>\r<br>sorry for late reply. \r<br>\r<br>I *think* I understood what you&#39;re asking for. As soon as you have added\r<br>a observation spesific intercept you&#39;re back in the Poisson world, and\r<br>you can do what you want. It is not wise to add something that is\r<br>confounded with the observation spesific intercept, but that is not what\r<br>you&#39;re thinking of doing. \r<br>\r<br>Best\r<br>H\r<br>\r<br>-- \r<br>Håvard Rue\r<br>Department of Mathematical Sciences\r<br>Norwegian University of Science and Technology\r<br>N-7491 Trondheim, Norway\r<br>Voice:  <a href=\"tel:+47%2073%2059%2035%2033\" value=\"+4773593533\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\">+47-7359-3533</a>    URL  : <a href=\"http://www.math.ntnu.no/~hrue\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.math.ntnu.no/~hrue&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792446011832000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2b7VNyA_U4D7dTqRYABQOa\">http://www.math.ntnu.no/~hrue</a>  \r<br>Mobile: <a href=\"tel:+47%2092%2060%2000%2021\" value=\"+4792600021\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\">+47-9260-0021</a>    Email: <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">havar...@math.ntnu.no</a>\r<br>\r<br>R-INLA: <a href=\"http://www.r-inla.org\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.r-inla.org&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792446011832000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2zoO1iffE4NtlxHwtfuUXj\">www.r-inla.org</a>\r<br>\r<br>"
    ]
